Part 1 — AWS EC2 Setup for Docker Learning

You first created an AWS IAM Admin user, then created an IAM role for EC2 with SSM access. After that:

Created an EC2 instance.
Attached the IAM role to the EC2 instance.
Connected to the EC2 instance using SSH.
Started running Linux commands.
First commands

1. sudo su

sudo = run a command with administrator/root privileges.
su = switch user.
sudo su therefore switches you to the root user.

You will usually see the prompt change from something like:

[ec2-user@ip-xxx ~]$

to:

[root@ip-xxx ~]#

The # indicates that you are operating as root.

Important: As root, you have very high privileges, so commands should be run carefully.

2. cd /

This is the command you were asking about.

cd = Change Directory
/ = the root directory of the Linux filesystem.

So:

cd /

means:

Go to the root directory of the Linux filesystem.

Think of / as the top-level folder of Linux.

For example:

/
├── bin
├── boot
├── dev
├── etc
├── home
├── opt
├── tmp
├── usr
└── var

You can check where you currently are with:

pwd

pwd = Print Working Directory

Example:

[root@ip-xxx ~]# pwd
/root

After:

cd /

you would get:

[root@ip-xxx /]# pwd
/
Quick memory trick
Command	Meaning
sudo	Run with elevated/admin privileges
su	Switch user
sudo su	Become root user
cd	Change directory
cd /	Go to Linux root directory
pwd	Show current directory
ls	List files/directories
